2.	Explain the difference between if and case statements in bash.
1. if Statement
•	Used to test conditions, such as numerical comparisons, string comparisons, or command exit statuses.
•	Supports multiple conditional expressions using elif and else.
•	Best for complex or range-based conditions.
Syntax:
if [ condition ]; then
    # commands if condition is true
elif [ another_condition ]; then
    # commands if another_condition is true
else
    # commands if all conditions are false
fi
2. case Statement
•	Used to match a value against multiple patterns.
•	Often preferred for fixed set of options like menu choices or letters.
•	Cleaner than multiple if…elif…else statements when testing discrete values.
Syntax:
case $variable in
pattern1)
    # commands
    ;;
pattern2)
    # commands
    ;;
*)
    # default commands
    ;;
esac

3.	Write a script to find the largest of three numbers entered by the user.
